+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,108 @@
+# Deploying Fizzy on a single server
+
+Fizzy runs happily on one Linux server. The included [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org/)
+configuration builds one container that serves the app, runs the Solid Queue
+background jobs, and keeps the SQLite databases and uploaded files in a Docker
+volume that survives future deploys. TLS is terminated by the proxy, which
+obtains and renews a certificate from Let's Encrypt for your hostname.
+
+You'll need:
+
+- A Linux server with a public IP address and SSH access (as `root`, by default).
+- A DNS record pointing your hostname at that server.
+- [Docker](https://docs.docker.com/get-docker/) on the machine you're deploying
+  from. Kamal installs Docker on the server for you.
+- Ports 80 and 443 open to the internet, so Let's Encrypt can issue a
+  certificate and visitors can reach the app over HTTPS.
+
+## Configure
+
+Everything you need to change lives in two files.
+
+### 1. `config/deploy.yml`
+
+Set your hostname and mail server. If you don't want to edit the file, the same
+values can be supplied as environment variables when you run Kamal.
+
+- `APP_HOST`: the hostname people will visit, e.g. `fizzy.example.com`. This is
+  also used to build links in outgoing email.
+- `DEPLOY_HOST`: the server to SSH into. Defaults to `APP_HOST`; set it to an IP
+  address if DNS isn't pointing at the server yet.
+- `SMTP_ADDRESS`, `SMTP_PORT`, `SMTP_USERNAME`, and `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS`: the
+  details for your mail server.
+- `builder.arch`: change to `arm64` if you're deploying to an ARM server.
+
+The config pushes the image to a local registry that Kamal manages for you, so
+there's no external registry or account to set up.
+
+### 2. `.kamal/secrets`
+
+Kamal reads your secrets from `.kamal/secrets`. Don't commit real secret values;
+keep them in your environment or a password manager and reference them here.
+
+- `SECRET_KEY_BASE`: Rails' secret key. Generate one once and keep it stable
+  across deploys, otherwise everyone gets logged out.
+  `export SECRET_KEY_BASE=$(openssl rand -hex 64)`
+- `SMTP_PASSWORD`: the password for the SMTP user configured above. Leave it
+  empty if your mail server doesn't require authentication.
+
+You can export both before deploying, or replace the references in the file with
+a command that reads them from your password manager, for example:
+
+```sh
+SECRET_KEY_BASE=$(op read "op://Private/Fizzy/SECRET_KEY_BASE")
+```
+
+## Deploy
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+This builds the image, pushes it to the local registry, boots `kamal-proxy` with
+a Let's Encrypt certificate for your hostname, and starts the container. On boot
+the entrypoint runs `db:prepare`, which creates or migrates the SQLite databases
+in the mounted volume.
+
+Fizzy is then available at `https://<your-hostname>`.
+
+For subsequent releases, just run:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+The `fizzy_storage` volume is mounted at `/rails/storage`, so the databases and
+uploaded files are reused rather than recreated.
+
+## Operating
+
+Some handy commands:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal app logs          # Tail the container logs
+bin/kamal console           # Open a Rails console
+bin/kamal shell             # Open a shell in the container
+bin/kamal app exec "bin/rails db:prepare"   # Manually run migrations
+```
+
+Background jobs are handled by Solid Queue inside the web process (see
+`config/puma.rb`), so there's no separate worker to manage.
+
+### Web push notifications (optional)
+
+If you want browser push notifications, generate a VAPID key pair and pass both
+values to the container. Add `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY` and `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY` to
+`env.secret` in `config/deploy.yml`, and provide them in `.kamal/secrets`:
+
+```ruby
+vapid_key = WebPush.generate_key
+puts "VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=#{vapid_key.private_key}"
+puts "VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=#{vapid_key.public_key}"
+```
+
+## Open source or SaaS?
+
+This config is for running open source Fizzy on your own server. 37signals'
+hosted service is a separate setup, bundled in the [`fizzy-saas`](../saas)
+engine. See `saas/README.md` if you're working on that.
